So we started off with a
very simple object, right?

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:25.990
It took two things.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.826
It says here's my code, run it.

00:11:27.826 --> 00:11:29.200
And here's some
conflict with it.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.700
And you can come
in and very easily

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.610
go ahead and iterate on
that, push some new code,

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.830
everything works fantastically--

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:37.510
almost fantastically.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.960
So underneath, what
we did is we took

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:44.080
that service object and we
created a configuration out

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:45.050
of that.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:49.330
So configuration is another
great practice that is taking

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.760
your software-- the code--

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.770
and the configuration
and putting them

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:57.820
together so that it's
a self-contained unit.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:11:59.830
This is a pretty
standard practice

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.380
from Twelve-Factor, and
putting together all your stuff

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.920
in one place, and making sure
that doesn't get out of sync.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.520
Whenever you make modifications
to the configuration object,

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.410
or when you create
one, underneath Knative

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.890
will go in and create
a revision out of that.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:17.370
So you interact with the
configuration object,

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.590
and underneath, Knative will go
in and make a revision object

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:21.190
for you.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.120
And whenever you
make a modification

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.930
to a configuration,
Knative will stamp out

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:27.640
a new version of revision.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.860
So at the end of the day,
for every modification

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:32.260
that you have made, you will
have a new version of it,

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.570
so you have a linear history
of all the changes that

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.370
are being applied to your code.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:39.880
So that's pretty nice, right?

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:40.940
What do I do with that?

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:42.490
Well, you probably want to
go ahead and put some traffic

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:42.940
into that.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.023
And that's where we have
in our out object, right?

00:12:45.023 --> 00:12:47.290
So we are now going ahead
and being able to come in

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.350
and saying, OK, great, I
have some software over there

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:52.720
that I built, and I can easily
go and make modifications

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:53.350
to it.

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:55.240
With the route object,
you can come in

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.550
and be very specific
about how traffic

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.280
flows into those objects.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.540
So again, let's just pause
there just for a second.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.140
So the roles and
responsibilities

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:06.110
are very clear here.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.110
So if you look at the
top of the box, service.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:09.700
That is very dev-opish, right?

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:11.320
You're basically
pushing a code in.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.090
Whenever you make a
change, it goes out

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:14.710
and starts serving traffic.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:17.241
And as you grow, you can
start seeing we are going down

00:13:17.241 --> 00:13:18.990
the stack a little
bit, where you can say,

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:22.600
hm, maybe I have a
clear operator role,

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:24.970
where the operator is
responsible for shuffling

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:25.930
my traffic.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.900
And the developer can go ahead
and worry about pushing code.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.510
So let's go and see
what that looks like.

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.910
So again, great on day one--

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.330
it was good to be in that
land of just push some code,

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:40.379
see it go live,
exciting, great times.

00:13:40.379 --> 00:13:41.920
Then you start taking
some customers,

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.253
and you might want to change
your practice a little bit.

00:13:44.253 --> 00:13:47.749
So let's see how
Knative grows with you.

00:13:47.749 --> 00:13:49.540
So here's what we are
going to do now here.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.610
We are just going to go through
a couple of slides together

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.380
here, just so we can
set the stage on what

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:54.630
it is that we are going to do.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.950
And then Mark is going
to demo it after then.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:00.610
But let's go ahead and
say, so this is roughly

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:03.250
what we had after we deployed
the very first version

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:04.150
of the software.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.090
So we have a route that is
shuffling 100% of the traffic

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:10.540
to the configuration that
only has one revision.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:11.440
That's all great.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.550
So we are only specifying that
100% of the traffic goes there.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:18.660
Now let's push some new code.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:19.240
OK?

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:20.890
But we don't want to
shift any traffic to it

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:22.180
because we probably
want to validate

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:23.221
that that new version is.

00:14:23.221 --> 00:14:25.450
So hopefully you
have some unit tests.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:27.190
Don't look at me, Scott.

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:30.980
But then you might have some
integration tests as well.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.460
And then you might want
to go ahead and test

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:36.130
your entire stack
against the new version

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:38.500
without affecting any of the
possible production traffic

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:39.660
that you have going on.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:40.390
OK?

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:43.640
So what we do here is we go
into Knative, and we say,

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.130
hey, route, what
I would like to do

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:47.800
is I actually would like
to create two targets.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.040
I'm going to go and create one
that is targeting my old one.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:53.590
And I'm going to go and create
a new one for my second one,

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:54.860
the blue green one.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:57.090
But I'm not going to
give it any traffic yet.

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:58.930
Oh, and also, please
give it a name

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:00.222
so I can talk about it, right?

00:15:00.222 --> 00:15:01.930
Because that name is
not really friendly,

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:04.100
so give it a fancy name.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.950
So push the new software,
no changes to your traffic.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.616
Let's see this.

00:15:09.616 --> 00:15:10.990
So then typically
you would maybe

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.120
start rolling out your
traffic a little bit.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:17.290
You might say, OK, well
typically maybe 2% experiment,

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:19.210
or 50%, or whatever else it is.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.160
But Mark likes to
live on the edge,

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:22.840
so he's just going
to go straight to 50.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.250
I told him he's silly,
but, you know, whatever.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:27.550
So now we are basically
going to go and say, great.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.302
Let's go and do
50-50 traffic split,

00:15:29.302 --> 00:15:31.510
just so we can see how things
work, because in a demo

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:34.300
it would be more
wonky to run at 2%.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.349
And then in the end, once
we validate everything,

00:15:36.349 --> 00:15:38.890
that's when we can go and shift
all of our production traffic

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:39.860
to the new version--

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.362
happy, making money.

00:15:41.362 --> 00:15:43.070
And then we can turn
off the old version.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.100
But we'll still leave
it around in case

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.360
we need to do a quick
little rollback.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.480
We can do that, so let's
call that version 1.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:51.460
So let's see what that looks
like on the demo machine,

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:51.959
please.

00:15:56.347 --> 00:15:58.680
MARK CHMARNY: All right, so
what are we going to do here

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.971
is we're going to kind of go
through the same process--

00:16:00.971 --> 00:16:01.920
oops, sorry.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.460
Blue-green, there we go--

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:04.580
same process.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:08.700
First of all, we're going to
deploy our basic application.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.790
We've showed you how
to do this before,

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:13.090
so I'm not going to talk
too much about that, just

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.100
to create the initial
state, what would happen

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:16.900
when you deploy your stack.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:21.580
So this is blue-green, and we're
going to talk about stage one.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.180
So let's deploy that.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:29.060
Wait for Knative to create
the instance of that,

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:30.790
all the necessary resources.

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:32.700
You see some activity
going on here.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:33.710
And magic happens.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:34.210
There we go.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:41.570
All right, so we have
our instance stood up.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:42.880
It runs.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.730
The day one, the joy of
deploying software is there.

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:47.810
Everything is great.

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.210
But then you say, well,
I would like my thing

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:53.540
to be something better or to
fix them some [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:57.230
So let's go over here and
take a look at the stage two.

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.320
Very much the same deployment,
another revision just

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.481
like we've done before,
another configuration,

00:17:02.481 --> 00:17:03.980
but in addition to
this, we also are

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:06.770
going to create that
Initial Routes tab

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.160
that Ville was talking about.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:13.220
So 100% to this old version,
0% to the new version.

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:15.524
Of course, deploying
application that

00:17:15.524 --> 00:17:17.690
doesn't have a way to access
to it would not be fun,

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:22.280
so we give it a name
route so we can get to it.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.550
So let's deploy the
stage two in here.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:29.870
Again, we're going to wait
for this to magically happen.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.900
All right, I can start
switching over here.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:41.364
And now what I can do is I
can continue refreshing this.

00:17:41.364 --> 00:17:42.780
You will see the
traffic continues

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:44.340
going to the original version.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:48.410
But the beauty of this is that
now we have this v2 in here

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:51.510
that I can also peek at, even
though 100% of the traffic

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:54.570
is not going-- none of the
traffic is going over there.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:57.480
All right, so now we've
done with revisions.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:00.540
We've basically deployed two
versions of the application.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.520
From now onwards,
all we're going to do

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.980
is we're just going to deal
with that route object in here.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.271
By the way, is this big enough?

00:18:06.271 --> 00:18:08.190
Everybody can see over there?

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:11.310
So in here, we're going to do
this very aggressive switching.

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:12.810
For the purposes
of demo, we're just

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:14.393
going to go 50-50
because I don't want

00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:17.590
hit Command-R too many times.

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:19.839
So let's apply this.

00:18:19.839 --> 00:18:23.270
Oops-- stage three--

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:28.250
actually, let's wait
for a second here.

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:31.280
It's all routes, so there
is nothing magical here.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:37.120
So going back to the
original traffic,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:40.330
50-50 is happening now.

00:18:42.842 --> 00:18:43.550
It's not science.

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:46.640
So it's round robin
of some sorts.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.330
And then now that we are
successful that that stage

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:53.250
of where we fixed everything
and we want to deploy,

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:57.680
we're shifting, or setting, the
traffic to the original version

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.970
to zero.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.085
We're migrating everything
to the new version.

00:19:01.085 --> 00:19:03.710
Oh, and by the way, just to kind
of have a historical reference

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:05.251
to get to the previous
version, we're

00:19:05.251 --> 00:19:08.120
going to name a new route that
will allow us to go to v1.

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.530
Again, no magic in here.

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:13.800
Let's finish this.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:19.030
Switch over here.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:20.790
Let's just wait for
this for a second.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.805
All right, there's still
a straggler in there.

00:19:24.805 --> 00:19:25.305
Nope.

00:19:28.894 --> 00:19:30.810
I'm pretty sure my crumb
is caching something.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.390
There we go.

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:35.830
100% of the traffic,
and we still can--

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.570
so you have seen with
V1-- with V2 before.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.340
We can still reach
to the old version

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.600
if we wanted to explicitly.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:45.510
All right, can you
switch back to slides?

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.785
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:51.320
VILLE AIKAS: All
right, so a couple

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:53.340
of things that are
interesting about that demo

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:54.840
that I'd really
wanted to point now,

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.209
which is as we started
with the original one, that

00:19:57.209 --> 00:19:59.000
was really easy to come
in and get started.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.230
And it was more like, again,
with the devops model,

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:02.229
where we just push code.

00:20:02.229 --> 00:20:03.260
Everything goes through.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.529
By introducing configuration
and route objects,

00:20:06.529 --> 00:20:08.570
now what you can do is
you can go ahead and start

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:11.360
defining not only roles
within your organization,

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.670
but also with these objects
by introducing things

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:14.540
like RBAC rules.

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:18.137
So you can say, OK devs, you
can push code all day you want.

00:20:18.137 --> 00:20:20.220
But none of it is going
to make it into production

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:23.360
because I will be in control
of the route object, which

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.040
seems nice.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.330
So OK, so that was great.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.330
So we talked about how
to get the code out there

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:29.960
in the cloud.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:31.490
Everything is hunky dory happy.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.096
But, Knative, what have you
done for me lately, right?

00:20:34.096 --> 00:20:35.720
So let's go ahead
and talk a little bit

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:38.300
about some of the other
aspects of serverless

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:42.290
that are typically
things that you

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.540
want to have out of the system.

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:45.920
So the first one
that we're going

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:51.069
to talk is the, when you leave
work, you're halfway to home,

00:20:51.069 --> 00:20:52.610
and then you realize,
oh my goodness,

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.380
did I leave the stove on, right?

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:56.870
So this is equivalent
of, you know,

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:58.614
did I just spin up a
whole massive stack

00:20:58.614 --> 00:21:00.780
that is going to be running
there the whole weekend,

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:03.560
burning cash, money flying out?

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.411
So, Knative, can you
take care of that for me?

00:21:06.411 --> 00:21:07.910
So one part of the
serverless is you

00:21:07.910 --> 00:21:09.320
don't think about serverless.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:12.730
So why would you care about
it if you left the stove on?

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:14.660
Knative should be able
to say, you know what?

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:15.530
It's not in use.

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:17.720
Let me go and put it away.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:18.800
That's great.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:20.180
The other way that
it handles is,

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:24.140
there's this very unbursty--

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:27.800
sorry, unbursty-- bursty--
that's bursted traffic, right?

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.900
Traffic pattern it's
not always like this.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:34.014
So your software makes it
into the Reddit or Hacker News

00:21:34.014 --> 00:21:35.930
or whatever the greatest
new thing is that you

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:36.987
find the software, right?

00:21:36.987 --> 00:21:38.570
So you're going to
get some new users.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:40.890
And you're going to bursted
traffic and everything else.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:43.348
You don't want to have to worry
about how many servers do I

00:21:43.348 --> 00:21:45.650
have running, and did
I bring up enough pods,

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:47.760
and did I do this and
that and everything else?

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:51.770
Knative should just handle
that for you as well.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:55.979
And also, it's easy to go
and take away resources

00:21:55.979 --> 00:21:57.020
when they are not in use.

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:58.460
It's not so easy to
go and bring them

00:21:58.460 --> 00:21:59.834
up when they
actually are needed.

00:21:59.834 --> 00:22:01.490
So going from one
to zero is actually

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:02.580
pretty straightforward.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:03.830
You just delete things.

00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:06.500
But it's more tricky to
go and bring them back

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:09.080
when they are actually needed
to go and service requests

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.340
when the traffic
actually comes back up.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:14.570
So let's go ahead and see how
Knative handles this for us,

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:17.021
kind of behind the scenes.

00:22:17.021 --> 00:22:18.770
Can we switch to the
demo machine, please?

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.480
MARK CHMARNY: The demo, please.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.100
Thank you.

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.600
All right so to do
this, we're going

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:32.190
to use the very
first application we

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.670
deployed during the first demo.

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:36.590
As you can see,
however, that demo

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.522
is not here because, thankfully,
I was afraid that some of you

00:22:39.522 --> 00:22:41.730
were going to go to the
browser and start hitting it.

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.410
Nobody went there, and therefore
there was no traffic to it.

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.040
So let's see what
would happen if I

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.595
start accessing this again.

00:22:48.595 --> 00:22:51.270
Knative, behind
the scene, start,

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:53.160
while holding the
traffic and kind

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:57.750
of waiting for the resource,
the necessary pod to be spun up,

00:22:57.750 --> 00:22:58.949
brought it in.

00:22:58.949 --> 00:23:00.240
And there is our demonstration.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:01.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:03.960
So this was the cold
start experience--

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.720
the famous cold start
that everybody talks about

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:08.960
in still very complex situation.

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:11.580
But Knative does
the first attempt

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.440
at solving that problem.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.995
Now I'm not going to
go here with a browser

00:23:15.995 --> 00:23:17.620
and started hitting
it a million times.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:19.230
We're going to go and
use a synthetic load--

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.646
VILLE AIKAS:
(WHISPERING) Cheater.

00:23:20.646 --> 00:23:23.070
MARK CHMARNY: [LAUGHS]
Cheater, yeah--

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:26.190
synthetic load to spin up
about 20 different threads

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:29.520
with 10,000 requests
for each one of those.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.290
And switch over here.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:34.170
Now Knative's going
to go and start

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:36.210
provisioning the necessary
pods and everything.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:38.690
Watching command
line and watching

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:42.390
Kubernetes provision pods is as
exciting as watching paint dry,

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:43.390
I guess.

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:46.890
So let's switch over to
an instance of our Grafana

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:49.260
here that comes with
Knative pre-configured.

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:51.420
It comes with a number
of different dashboards

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:53.790
that the Knative team,
K-native native team,

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:56.310
has provisioned
nicely for us here.

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:57.490
I'm going to use two ones.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:02.860
First of all, we're going to
take a look at the traffic.

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:06.000
Let's maybe go a
little to future here.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.274
You will see three lines.

00:24:07.274 --> 00:24:09.690
I'm colorblind, so I'm going
to tell you what they told me

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:10.773
what the colors look like.

00:24:10.773 --> 00:24:15.270
But we will see the
green line, which

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:16.650
is the actual number of pods.

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:20.010
We will see the desired number
of pods, what Knative thinks

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:22.710
you should have in order
to solve that traffic,

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:24.930
and the number of requested.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.990
And you will see that, over us--

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.006
let's switch over
here very quickly.

00:24:30.006 --> 00:24:32.990
I'll show you the number of pods
that Knative spun here for you.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:38.500
There we go.

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:41.370
There's some activity
going on, I would say.

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:46.570
And refreshing-- we see that
this kind of what do you want,

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:47.820
what you get, and go on.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.870
In addition to that, you can
also use a requester dashboard

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:52.840
in here.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.350
Hopefully it choose
the right application.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:56.170
And refresh this.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:03.840
Why are we not seeing
any traffic in here?

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.555
Well, there is the first
demo crash I think.

00:25:06.555 --> 00:25:08.930
All right, we should see the
number of requests coming in

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:09.800
for that request.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:10.865
I'm using simple.

00:25:10.865 --> 00:25:15.860
Oh, it might be something with
our mice, my time in here.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:19.320
All right, hopefully the
other one was better.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:20.410
There we go.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.412
VILLE AIKAS: (WHISPERING)
Way to break it.

00:25:22.412 --> 00:25:23.870
MARK CHMARNY:
Already broke a demo.

00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:25.880
Now the other thing
that's going to go on,

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:29.270
if as soon as the demo
finishes, which it's done,

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:32.410
you will see the Knative
start decommissioning

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:34.180
the no-longer-necessary pods.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:35.750
So we'll go in and start--

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:36.294
there we go.

00:25:36.294 --> 00:25:38.710
I was hoping for that to happen,
just like Ryan yesterday.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.820
I got just as lucky
as he did yesterday--

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:45.390
[INAUDIBLE] the pods as the
requests starts coming in.

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:50.810
Can we change back to slides?

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:52.480
Sorry, we warned
you there was going

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:54.859
to be a lot of switching there.

00:25:54.859 --> 00:25:56.400
VILLE AIKAS: So that
was pretty nice.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.024
And there was a couple
of things that I

00:25:58.024 --> 00:25:59.280
don't know if somebody caught.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:02.580
So you could see how the
blue-green deployments

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:03.957
were going away as well.

00:26:03.957 --> 00:26:05.790
So those things are all
configurable for you

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:09.690
so you can decide what is the
sleep-off, how long things hang

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:11.850
around before they are
considered obsolete-- well,

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:16.194
not obsolete, but before we
can put them to bed for a bit.

00:26:16.194 --> 00:26:16.860
So that's great.

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:18.240
So now we have sort
of kind of shown you

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.070
how you can go and get
the software on the cloud,

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.450
and manage it, and
things like that.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:23.490
And we've sort of kind
of shown you how Knative

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.590
comes in and takes
away some of the things

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:28.380
that you really don't care
about in the serverless land.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:32.460
Also, what Knative does
for you is handling going

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:34.530
from source to code, right?

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:37.530
So if you caught all the
examples we showed you so far,

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:40.950
we're just taking a pointer
to a container, right?

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:44.220
So again, as a
developer, I typically

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:45.454
don't care about that.

00:26:45.454 --> 00:26:46.870
I just want to
write the software.

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:49.416
And I want to say, run the
software, not the container.

00:26:49.416 --> 00:26:50.540
There's a difference there.

00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:52.560
Just run my code.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:56.100
So we also have a building
repo that allows you to come in

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.260
and say, OK my
code is over there

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:01.494
in the Git, or Cloud Storage,
or something like that.

00:27:01.494 --> 00:27:03.660
And you can come in and
say, please, take that code.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:04.624
Just run it.

00:27:04.624 --> 00:27:05.790
I don't care about the bill.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:06.510
Do it.

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:09.420
So why do you, as
a developer, care?

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:12.830
Well, again, once you go
and get up your laptop,

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.830
all the tools are installed
and everything else,

00:27:14.830 --> 00:27:16.299
and the project
grows a little bit,

00:27:16.299 --> 00:27:18.090
then you get your friend
working on it too,

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:19.470
and they have a
different laptop.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.960
And then you start
getting all those fun

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:24.150
cross-compiling problems,
and the cross image,

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:25.272
and nothing works anymore.

00:27:25.272 --> 00:27:27.730
So now you can go and have an
easy way to go ahead and say,

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:28.313
you know what?

00:27:28.313 --> 00:27:30.300
Let's do the build
one way just once.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.030
That seems better, right?

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:35.040
There's also no need for
Docker locally, right?

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:36.650
So that's nice.

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:38.510
Less things I need
to do on my machine,

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:40.260
less things I need to
upgrade, less things

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:41.460
I need to keep running.

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:43.260
Great, perfect, right?

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:46.267
Also, there's performance
improvements out of this one.

00:27:46.267 --> 00:27:47.850
Because when I'm
running in the cloud,

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.599
I can take advantage
of the cloud caching.

00:27:49.599 --> 00:27:52.830
So typically the machines in the
cloud have much bigger pipes.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.470
So they can suck bytes
in and out faster, right?

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.600
I can also take advantage
of caching behavior

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:00.910
of things like image layers.

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:02.730
So if you're building
something, chances

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:06.000
are somebody else is building at
least lower, middle-level parts

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.224
of it, and your code
goes on the top there.

00:28:08.224 --> 00:28:10.140
And then the nice thing
is that because it all

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:13.200
happens in the cloud, now you
can go start creating things

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.190
like audit logs.

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:15.690
You can go and add
additional things

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:19.714
into this tool chain that allows
you to go ahead and say, hm,

00:28:19.714 --> 00:28:20.880
who did what, when, and why?

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:26.744
And again, this is something
that is loosely coupled.

00:28:26.744 --> 00:28:28.410
So one of the things
we don't want to do

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:31.050
is we don't want to come in and
say, in order to use Knative,

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:33.890
you must do things this
way every single time.

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:35.670
So in the first
examples we showed you,

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:37.262
bring your own toolchain, right?

00:28:37.262 --> 00:28:39.720
So many of you might already
have a CI/CD pipeline running.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:40.480
Great.

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:41.820
Everybody's happy.

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:44.070
But this allows you to also
go and say, you know what?

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:45.720
I don't really
necessarily need that.

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:47.340
Let's just go ahead
and use this one.

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:49.200
Or if you're coming
in from the otherland,

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:50.634
where you are
running into GitHub

00:28:50.634 --> 00:28:52.050
and building on
the local machine,

00:28:52.050 --> 00:28:53.750
it allows you to
move into the land

00:28:53.750 --> 00:28:55.350
where you're coming in and
saying like, you know what?

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:56.650
Just handle it for me.

00:28:56.650 --> 00:28:59.250
And then maybe out of that you
graduate again to some CI/CD

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:00.840
pipeline and whatever else.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.624
And that's all fine and dandy.

00:29:02.624 --> 00:29:04.290
But again, these are
the kinds of things

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.390
that Knative does for
you automagically,

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.132
giving you that serverless
thing, serverless kind of--

00:29:09.132 --> 00:29:11.590
I don't want to call it promise,
but let's call it promise.

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:15.880
It's just like going from source
to running code, that's great.

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:16.560
There it is.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.680
And also, the, hey, I don't
worry about the infrastructure,

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:19.180
right?

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:21.330
So so far we haven't done
anything here dealing

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.330
with machines or
lower-level details,

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.210
except when we actually go look
and make sure that something

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:28.320
is actually happening.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:30.319
It would be a boring demo
if we didn't do that.

00:29:30.319 --> 00:29:31.860
So we just kind of
show those to you.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:34.060
But you don't have
to worry about that.

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.463
So let's look at the next slide.

00:29:36.463 --> 00:29:38.330
Oops, sorry.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:39.045
OK.

00:29:39.045 --> 00:29:40.380
MARK CHMARNY: Demo.

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:41.290
VILLE AIKAS: Yes.

00:29:41.290 --> 00:29:43.666
So let's switch to the demo
and see what this looks like.

00:29:43.666 --> 00:29:44.831
MARK CHMARNY: OK, thank you.

00:29:44.831 --> 00:29:46.470
So what we're going
to do here, we're

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:49.640
going to take the
traditional GitHub operation.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:52.830
So we're going to take GitHub
repository or Git Repository

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:54.080
hosted on GitHub.

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:55.820
We're going to, in
this case, we're

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:59.640
going to follow a very
simple application that

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.830
probably is as simple as
one could create a web

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:02.670
server with in Go.

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:05.600
But I'm sure there's
some room to improve it.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:08.600
And it has a Docker
file in here to specify

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:10.326
how I would like to build this.

00:30:10.326 --> 00:30:12.450
Obviously, this would apply
for different languages

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:14.070
in different kind
of formats there.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:19.120
So what we're going to do
here is let's fire this job

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:20.670
so we can talk about it.

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:24.955
Source-- all right,
so while this is--

00:30:24.955 --> 00:30:27.080
while Knative is spinning
up the resources,

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:28.830
let's take a look at
the manifest that was

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:30.730
used in this particular case.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:34.270
Again, we're building
from GitHub repo.

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:37.660
Knative supports both public
repositories as well as secrets

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:39.640
for private repos,
so you don't have

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.779
to be dependent on exposing
the entire thing to the world.

00:30:42.779 --> 00:30:45.070
And we're going to have a
couple of components in here.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:47.350
First of all is the
configuration component

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:53.270
of the build process, which
will include the source bit,

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.770
which will indicate the
source of the source,

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:59.369
as in the repository from
which we want to pull,

00:30:59.369 --> 00:31:01.160
as well as the branch
we want to pull from.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.119
So it supports
multiple branches.

00:31:03.119 --> 00:31:04.910
In addition to this,
we're going to specify

00:31:04.910 --> 00:31:06.260
a particular template.

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:08.500
Knative supports a number
of different templates.

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:10.510
We're going to use, in
this case, a cononical,

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:12.340
which is an open
source project that's

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:16.870
also already public,
to create an image that

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:23.030
will be pushed when done
to a image repository.

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:25.470
Knative also provide
certain kind of shortcuts.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:28.510
So we're going to define a
variable in here, called image,

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:30.440
that will hold this URL.

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:32.260
So in the rest of
the configuration,

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:34.550
we can just use that variable.

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:36.550
The beauty of this is
that when we come in here,

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:38.440
when we start talking
about the actual target

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:41.170
of the deployment, we're going
to specify we want a target--

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:43.090
deploy this as an application.

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:45.670
We want to use the
URL of the image

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:48.147
once was published to
the image repository.

00:31:48.147 --> 00:31:50.230
And we're also are going
to define the environment

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:52.394
variables that we've
talked about before,

00:31:52.394 --> 00:31:54.310
the same way you would
have done for any other

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:55.760
of the application.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:58.290
So let's take a look
at where we are.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:03.280
Looks like image is still
being built. What Knative does

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:06.110
is has a multi-phase
process involved in here.

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:09.050
So it, first of all, pulls
all the necessary resources.

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:11.800
It does certain months
of configuration in it.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:13.600
Then once the image
is built, publishes

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:16.780
the image then turns around
and uses the image just like it

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:19.750
did if you were building,
deploying an application

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.130
from a published image.

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:24.410
In this case, we're seeing a--

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:28.262
two of three, almost there.

00:32:28.262 --> 00:32:29.220
There we go.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:32.410
So let's take a look at this--

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:33.335
what is it?

00:32:33.335 --> 00:32:33.960
--source image.

00:32:37.152 --> 00:32:38.471
And there we go.

00:32:38.471 --> 00:32:40.826
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:43.320
VILLE AIKAS: All right.

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:46.730
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:47.230
OK.

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:48.392
So the nice thing about--

00:32:48.392 --> 00:32:50.350
one additional thing I
wanted to point out here

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:53.350
is that another reason why
you might want to about this

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:57.237
is this is totally
pluggable, so that you

00:32:57.237 --> 00:32:59.570
can go ahead and have different
kinds of pipelines here.

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:03.010
So, for example, some people
have built things like .Net

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:03.650
pipelines.

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:05.608
So you can go and build
things like that there.

00:33:05.608 --> 00:33:07.830
There's build-back
support and so forth.

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:10.000
So that's also very
nice and customizable.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:14.020
So it's not only just Docker
thingies that we built.

00:33:14.020 --> 00:33:15.730
All right, so, great.

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:17.620
So so far again, we've
talked a little bit

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:19.930
about some aspects
of serverless.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:22.990
But sometimes it seems like--

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:26.329
MARK CHMARNY: What has come
to be the norm [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:26.329 --> 00:33:26.871
There you go.

00:33:26.871 --> 00:33:27.912
VILLE AIKAS: There we go.

00:33:27.912 --> 00:33:28.490
Am I there?

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:29.700
Yeah, OK, great.

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:30.810
I am here, yes.

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:35.700
OK, so it seems that
over the last few years,

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.150
it seems like what has come
to be the norm for serverless

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:43.410
is serverless equals,
equals, equals functions.

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:45.180
That's all it is, right?

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.765
Well, with Knative,
there's bigger things

00:33:47.765 --> 00:33:49.140
that we are worrying
about, which

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.070
is serverless is much,
much bigger than functions.

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:54.290
So we've been doing
serverless for a long time

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:57.180
with App Engine, so we are kind
of like trying to bring the two

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:58.980
things together.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.910
However, the one nice
thing about the serverless,

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:05.460
the programming
model that typically

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:09.679
resonates with most developers
is the eventing-driven one.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:12.989
So I have written some
software, and when

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:15.989
I hit it with the browser,
that is still event-drive.

00:34:15.989 --> 00:34:19.110
I'm coming in and saying,
could you do something for me?

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:20.790
But what we have
done with the Knative

00:34:20.790 --> 00:34:23.670
is really given a
first-class principal

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:29.159
objects for describing where
those events are coming from.

00:34:29.159 --> 00:34:32.790
So on the bottom right there,
we have the event consumers.

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:34.500
So those are the things
that we have been

00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:36.520
deploying all day long so far.

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:38.520
So there's nothing different
about those things.

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:41.120
We are just pushing
code, rolling it forward,

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:43.090
doing our testing,
and everything else.

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:45.960
So those are the
event consumers OK?

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.480
On the top right, we
have the event sources.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:51.100
So something is producing
events for me, right?

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.520
So this might be, for
example, a GitHub.

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:57.270
So GitHub has a very
rich eventing source.

00:34:57.270 --> 00:34:59.640
Kubernetes cluster itself
is very, very rich.

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:02.490
Anything that happens in the
cluster, chances are it's

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:04.470
pushing out events.

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:05.890
For each one of
the event sources,

00:35:05.890 --> 00:35:08.646
there's typically more
than one event type,

00:35:08.646 --> 00:35:10.020
so the kind of
event it supports.

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:13.035
So, for example, the GitHub,
you have a pull request.

00:35:13.035 --> 00:35:14.410
So if somebody
comes in and says,

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.237
hey, here's a pull request,
or if I make a commit,

00:35:17.237 --> 00:35:19.445
those two events are very
different in their context.

00:35:22.060 --> 00:35:24.660
And so there is this
one-to-many relationship

00:35:24.660 --> 00:35:28.470
in Knative for event
sources and event types.

00:35:28.470 --> 00:35:31.020
And so what is
left then is, OK, I

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:32.790
have those event
types and sources,

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:34.210
and I have my code here.

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:35.520
How do I wire them together?

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:37.670
And for that there is
an object called AFLOW.

00:35:37.670 --> 00:35:40.560
So with the flow,
you go ahead and say,

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:42.570
Mr. Knative, go over there.

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:46.560
Take those events from there
and wire them over to here.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:49.110
So let's go and see what
this looks like in practice

00:35:49.110 --> 00:35:50.940
if we were to go ahead and--

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:54.150
to see how seamless it
is to go ahead and go

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:56.520
from the application
during programming or just

00:35:56.520 --> 00:36:00.210
the pure container model, to
the function and programming,

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:01.770
using the same tool sets.

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:03.895
Can we switch the
demo machine, please?

00:36:03.895 --> 00:36:05.895
MARK CHMARNY: Almost done,
couple more switches.

00:36:05.895 --> 00:36:08.280
Right, so to illustrate
this, what we're going to do

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:11.580
is we're actually going to
wire the Knative into an event

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:13.920
stream that's coming
from IoT devices.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:18.660
Google has a product called IoT
Core that provides an easy way,

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:21.300
like a gateway, for registering
devices and securely

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:23.560
managing them with keys.

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:26.400
So what we've done here is we've
created one of those gateways.

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:28.020
No code required,
it's just simply

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:31.600
a GUI operation that defined
two different types of protocols

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:34.650
we're going to be receiving
on, MPTT and HTTP,

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:38.070
define our mocked-up
IoT device, because I

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:41.250
couldn't get one of those
from our team over there.

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:43.930
And it's called Net 18--

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:45.450
sorry, Next 18.

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:46.560
And we're also going to--

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:49.507
by default, what
IoT Core does, it

00:36:49.507 --> 00:36:52.440
create a like sync
for all those events.

00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:55.005
So in this case, we're going
to have two topics on Pub/Sub.

00:36:55.005 --> 00:36:58.260
Pub/Sub is the message
broker in Google Cloud.

00:36:58.260 --> 00:37:01.810
That one is for IoT data,
so that data itself.

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:04.650
The other one is metadata
about the devices that connect.

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.960
So you can event on
things like my device has

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:10.030
connected or disconnected,
or a certain threshold.

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:12.090
So this is our source of events.

00:37:12.090 --> 00:37:14.110
What are we going to do here?

00:37:14.110 --> 00:37:17.490
We're going to go in and
switch quickly and take a look

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.530
at the actual code itself.

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:23.292
Now event-flow, there we go.

00:37:23.292 --> 00:37:24.750
Let's take a look
at first the code

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:26.130
that we used to publish it.

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:27.720
All we've done here--

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:31.090
yeah, we have some Go, Go
things like defining the types.

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:33.810
But bottom line is, we've
done one function that

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.720
handles the events that are
going to be passed to me.

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:39.690
The cool thing about it is
that, in this entire code,

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:44.010
there's no references to Pub/Sub
or any proprietary Google code.

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:47.700
You wrote idiomatic Go
language that receives events.

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:48.600
This works for Node.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:51.090
This works for any other
language you can support it

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.890
within the runtime on Knative.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:54.351
And when you do this--

00:37:54.351 --> 00:37:56.100
again, we're not going
do any fancy thing.

00:37:56.100 --> 00:37:58.433
So we're basically just going
to print this entire thing

00:37:58.433 --> 00:38:03.460
to command line and see
what's going to happen.

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:06.990
So let's take a look at
the flow, the configuration

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:08.370
portion of the entire thing.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:11.290
Similarly to all the other way
with configuring applications,

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:15.150
we're going to have a
principal object that's

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:17.810
defined by Knative,
the flow object, that

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:20.530
defines the name and all
the other parameters.

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:21.790
And it has two components.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:23.180
One is the trigger,
as in what do

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:25.560
we want to connect to
from the source of events.

00:38:25.560 --> 00:38:29.690
It includes the name of the
project, as well as the topic,

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:31.280
as well as the
connection that is

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:34.600
the target for where the
events should be processed for.

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.376
Nothing new in here,
we're deploying

00:38:36.376 --> 00:38:37.750
a simple function,
which is going

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:39.520
to be representing the route.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:40.840
So let's go and apply this.

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:49.700
Up-- flow-- there we go.

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:54.340
We should see some additional
resources being created,

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:55.320
I think.

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:05.500
Oh, that's great.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:06.330
That's just great.

