00:40:56.080 --> 00:40:57.310
that Mark wrote.

00:40:57.310 --> 00:41:00.502
But again, we have
deployed that previously

00:41:00.502 --> 00:41:02.710
because one of the concepts
of wanting to demonstrate

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:05.029
is the notion of late binding.

00:41:05.029 --> 00:41:06.820
So you want to go and
define your function.

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:08.140
You want to run the function.

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:10.180
You want to go ahead
and roll that forward,

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:13.480
independent of how it is
wired to something else.

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:15.080
So the function is
happily running.

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:16.930
Notice it was
sleeping there, right?

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:18.460
It was getting no traffic.

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:21.760
And then it came up when
we started getting that.

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:24.170
And the Pub/Sub event
source and the topic

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.460
were our event
source's name and types

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.350
in this particular instance.

00:41:29.350 --> 00:41:31.600
Those are user defined also.

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:35.530
So you can come in if you have
some existing systems that you

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:36.700
want to go and wire in.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:38.866
There's a way to come in
there and extend the system

00:41:38.866 --> 00:41:42.145
by providing glue logic to say,
OK, this is how you go ahead

00:41:42.145 --> 00:41:44.920
and configure things.

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:48.610
And the topics of subscription
that Mark showed for the flow

00:41:48.610 --> 00:41:49.690
was precisely that.

00:41:49.690 --> 00:41:51.500
It says, there's
the GCP Pub/Sub.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.620
I want to go and get
events from this topic.

00:41:53.620 --> 00:41:55.967
And I want them wired
into this Go function.

00:41:55.967 --> 00:41:57.050
So that was pretty simple.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.050
Again, it was very,
very nice way.

00:41:59.050 --> 00:42:02.050
What we didn't show you there
is that the nice thing is

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:03.620
there's a clear lifecycle here.

00:42:03.620 --> 00:42:06.172
So once you come in and you
delete that flow object,

00:42:06.172 --> 00:42:07.630
it's going to go
away and clean up.

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:11.180
So, for example, Pub/Sub
resources are deleted.

00:42:11.180 --> 00:42:13.540
And again, it decouples
the even production

00:42:13.540 --> 00:42:17.440
from the consumer, which
is a very nice property.

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:22.030
Now underneath again,
there's a lot of complexity

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.160
that we don't get a
chance to show here.

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:26.560
But there is
additional resources

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:29.500
that typically are facing
the operator persona here,

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:31.240
so things like
channels and buses

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:34.804
so that the traffic is shuffling
through there and so forth.

00:42:34.804 --> 00:42:36.220
But again, because
we are focusing

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:39.370
on what the developer
sees, this as the developer

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:42.430
experience right here.

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:46.630
We are really piggybacking off
of CNC of cloud event support.

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:48.730
So a lot of our
friends at Google

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:50.380
and colleagues over
at other companies

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:52.780
have been working on
standardizing the cloud events,

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:56.950
trying to make sure that, in
the near future, that there's

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:00.065
standardization on what
flows on the wire there

00:43:00.065 --> 00:43:03.070
and how do you consume
events and things like that.

00:43:03.070 --> 00:43:04.730
So we are using that.

00:43:04.730 --> 00:43:07.750
And if you looked at the
code, we have Go types,

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:09.850
and that's because,
well, that code

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:11.200
was written a little while ago.

00:43:11.200 --> 00:43:14.260
By now there is a cloud
of end support there too.

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:15.460
So, yay.

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:18.220
But anyways, so that's
our eventing story.

