00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:30.990
the developer has to do
every day hundreds of times

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:33.640
if they're really
developing, is handle for you

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:34.890
by Knative automatic--

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:37.030
K-native automatically.

00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:38.260
Knative is also extendable.

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:41.890
Now any software that
anybody has ever written,

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:45.640
implements opinions,
opinions of the developer.

00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:46.850
Knative is no different.

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:50.500
It implements
opinions that we have,

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:53.365
based on the years of learning
from developing software

00:44:53.365 --> 00:44:55.890
at Google, at the scale
that Google operates,

00:44:55.890 --> 00:45:00.010
including implements opinions
of the partners and contributors

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:01.570
to the project.

00:45:01.570 --> 00:45:04.180
But from the very beginning,
it was very important for us

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:08.620
that these opinions are not
held very, very tightly,

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:12.040
that we can change those
as the use cases come up.

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:15.189
Serverless is still very
beginning stages, frankly,

00:45:15.189 --> 00:45:16.480
at very, very beginning stages.

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:17.439
And things will evolve.

00:45:17.439 --> 00:45:19.896
So we want to make sure that
those opinions can be replaced

00:45:19.896 --> 00:45:21.320
when to use case
you're working on

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:24.950
is not being served by the
capabilities at Knative.

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:28.350
So a lot of that is built,
is baked right into the API.

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:30.160
That means event
sources and types,

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:33.670
I think if you go to our
GitHub repository today,

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:37.270
you will see
examples for Pub/Sub.

00:45:37.270 --> 00:45:38.200
You will see GitHub.

00:45:38.200 --> 00:45:39.700
You will see a
couple of other ones.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:41.720
But it is not a large
portfolio of events.

00:45:41.720 --> 00:45:43.750
We are going to
get better there.

00:45:43.750 --> 00:45:47.650
It includes support for a
number of different build packs.

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.100
The Pivotal team
has, for example,

00:45:49.100 --> 00:45:53.450
provided a build pack for C#
that we have not developed.

00:45:53.450 --> 00:45:55.340
So you can extend
those and configure

00:45:55.340 --> 00:45:57.614
this post-installation
on Knative.

00:45:57.614 --> 00:45:59.405
It includes a lot of
network configuration.

00:45:59.405 --> 00:46:01.010
So, for example, for
the demonstration

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:03.120
today, we are using GCP gateway.

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:06.530
We're using SSL
configuration that

00:46:06.530 --> 00:46:10.550
was enabled us to buy Knative
IP configuration for static IP,

00:46:10.550 --> 00:46:13.490
as number of different variables
they're logging and tagging.

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:17.870
Now Knative, K-native,
not obfuscate Kubernetes.

00:46:17.870 --> 00:46:19.340
Underneath it's
still Kubernetes.

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:20.820
And you have still access to it.

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:22.880
In fact, I was using
the very same tool

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.530
you're using to manage
Kubernetes itself.

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:28.137
So if you feel like,
you could come in

00:46:28.137 --> 00:46:29.720
and change some of
the implementations

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:31.928
that we've done, including
the auto-scaling strategy.

00:46:31.928 --> 00:46:34.790
I showed you auto-scaling
based on number of requests.

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:36.890
You could base this on
latency to connection

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:38.787
to the database or
something like that.

00:46:38.787 --> 00:46:41.120
I don't recommending doing
that on the first day playing

00:46:41.120 --> 00:46:43.060
with Knative, but you could.

00:46:43.060 --> 00:46:45.950
Functions invokers--
the community at large

00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:49.590
is working on the best way
of implementing this for Go,

00:46:49.590 --> 00:46:53.862
or Python, or Node.js,
different message boxes

00:46:53.862 --> 00:46:54.820
that you can implement.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:59.210
There is a lot of different
customization options.

00:46:59.210 --> 00:47:01.240
All right, we have
three minutes to go.

00:47:01.240 --> 00:47:03.240
And we do want to leave
some time for questions.

00:47:03.240 --> 00:47:06.540
So there's a couple
of links in here.

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.600
First of all, everything
we've shown you today

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:09.440
is reproducible.

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:14.180
If you go to our documents
repository in Knative.org.

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:15.860
You can get there,
start from there,

00:47:15.860 --> 00:47:17.726
and it will navigate
to everywhere else.

00:47:17.726 --> 00:47:19.100
You could also,
if you don't want

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:22.310
to play the role of a operator,
which for the purposes of demo

00:47:22.310 --> 00:47:25.400
I was playing, we have a
sign-on for early access

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:27.530
to serverless
add-on on GKE, which

00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:29.810
will do all of that for
you with a single command.

00:47:29.810 --> 00:47:32.540
Give me Knative-- boom, done.

00:47:32.540 --> 00:47:35.000
Or give me a
serverless stack, which

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:38.180
is kind of using the components
that were released later

00:47:38.180 --> 00:47:40.010
as open source for Knative.

00:47:40.010 --> 00:47:42.180
We also have a pretty
vibrant community already.

00:47:42.180 --> 00:47:43.640
So if you looked at it
and go, you know what?

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:44.540
This sounds interesting.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:45.620
I would love to contribute.

00:47:45.620 --> 00:47:47.270
Or these guys don't
know what they're doing.

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:47.936
Let me tell you.

00:47:47.936 --> 00:47:50.040
Let me show you how
it's really done.

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:53.639
All right, come in here,
the community inside

00:47:53.639 --> 00:47:55.430
of our repository is
a good place to start.

00:47:55.430 --> 00:47:57.470
It talks about the
code of conduct,

00:47:57.470 --> 00:47:59.570
talks about the kind of
how it gets you started.

00:47:59.570 --> 00:48:03.335
It even highlights best
to start with issues

00:48:03.335 --> 00:48:04.460
that are already out there.

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:06.690
And trust me, there's
a bunch of devs there.

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:08.420
And as any hip
project nowadays, we

00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:11.030
have a Slack
channel that's open.

00:48:11.030 --> 00:48:14.520
Not sure that exact
